<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baghdad Pact</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1955">1955</a>–<a href="/years/1979">1979</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Central Treaty Organization, CENTO</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> Pact was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> defensive alliance formed in 1955 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> and the United Kingdom, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> closely associated. It aimed to contain Soviet influence in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>. After the 1958 revolution Iraq withdrew in 1959, and the alliance was renamed the Central Treaty Organization, which was dissolved in 1979.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baghdad-pact.jpg" alt="Baghdad Pact" width="320" height="162" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Little Professor · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cento_zoom.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Central Treaty Organization, also called the Baghdad Pact and at first the Middle East Treaty Organization, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> military alliance formed on 24 February 1955 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and the United Kingdom. It was modelled on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">North Atlantic Treaty Organization</a> and committed its members to mutual cooperation and protection and to non-intervention in each other's affairs. Its aim was to contain the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> by a line of strong states along its south-western frontier, which gave it the name Northern Tier. American pressure and promises of aid were key in the negotiations, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> could not at first take part.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Unlike <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a>, the pact had no unified military command, and few American or British bases were established in member countries. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> did have communications and intelligence facilities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and flew U-2 flights over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> from bases in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. The headquarters were in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> from 1955 to 1958 and then in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a> until 1979. In 1958 the United States joined the military committee.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> had signed a Pact of Mutual Cooperation with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> in February 1954. A secretary general served renewable three-year terms. The organisation also sponsored research and cultural bodies such as the CENTO Institute of Nuclear Science, which moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> in 1958, and a railway from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-van">Lake Van</a> in Turkey to Sharafkhaneh in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, funded largely by the United Kingdom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>On 14 July 1958 the Iraqi monarchy was overthrown in a military coup, and the new government under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-karim-qasim">Abd al-Karim Qasim</a> withdrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> from the pact, which formally ended on 24 March 1959, and adopted a non-aligned stance. The organisation then took the name CENTO. In the 1960s it declined to become involved in the Arab-Israeli conflict or the Indo-Pakistani wars: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>'s requests for help in 1965 and 1971 were rejected on the ground that the alliance aimed to contain the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, not <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><p>After the Turkish invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> in 1974 the United Kingdom withdrew the forces earmarked for the alliance, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-congress">United States Congress</a> halted military aid to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. Iran withdrew on 11 March 1979 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> the next day, and on 16 March 1979 the organisation was formally dissolved. Later defence agreements between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> or the United Kingdom and regional states were made bilaterally.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Central Treaty Organization” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pakistan’s efforts in the 1950s were complicated by its part in the <b>Baghdad Pact</b> and its pro-Western diplomacy, and many Muslim countries suspected that Pakistan aspired to leadership of the Muslim world in order to help Western powers in their relations with other Muslim states.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/pan-islamism">Pan-Islamism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1955 Iraq joined the <b>Baghdad Pact</b>, and in 1958 it formed the Arab Federation with Jordan.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-iraq">Kingdom of Iraq</a></li><li><blockquote>Qasim left the <b>Baghdad Pact</b>, began land reform and claimed Kuwait, but was killed in a Ba'athist coup in 1963.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-iraq">Republic of Iraq</a></li></ul></section>
